f 17592186045417] [17592186050527 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050527 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050527 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186161965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050527 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186161966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050527 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186161967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050527 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186161968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050527 #edb/ref 1031 "Creating Rows or Columns Containing Zeros Using Gaussian Elimination" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050527 #edb/ref 1033 3029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050527 #edb/ref 1034 0.2853007808149399 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050527 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "508ac00e-0867-53e8-a192-d981ff4bbdc2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050576 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050576 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050576 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050576 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050576 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050576 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428318 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050576 #edb/ref 1031 "Elementary 2x2 Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050576 #edb/ref 1033 1726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050576 #edb/ref 1034 0.639102274928378 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050576 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "54fffdff-21ee-5191-90e6-7d1d444eefd6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050765 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050765 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050765 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050765 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050765 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050765 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050765 #edb/ref 1031 "Systems of Equations as Augmented Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050765 #edb/ref 1033 148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050765 #edb/ref 1034 0.6027738609637455 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050765 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "64dbabd7-08e6-5435-b006-ab4997312f47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113983 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113983 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113983 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113983 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113983 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113983 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113983 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113983 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186550776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186113983 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4eb2360f-9c37-5242-a36d-bf3f7078ccc0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115072 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4716" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115072 #edb/ref 1071 "Is \n$\\begin{aligned}E=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$ \nan elementary matrix?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186115072 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "239ee94c-c3d9-5955-8cdd-afff77ec3279" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115072 #edb/ref 1074 "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation.\n\nThe given matrix $E$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I_2$ by applying the elementary row operation $R_2:= R_2+(-5) R_1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186115073 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4718" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115073 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n![](images/03/03d58fbe64af980e170a695b5f511df7e14cd74cc894e802ce5d781642d58b2b.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115073 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "40cdd39f-29c3-5dad-9708-02c3e0de6acb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115073 #edb/ref 1074 "We write down $E$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n![](images/7b/7bb507644e3602983ed613dbe3b630fbd4fe72799c5412272a072a39c736cbc5.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186115074 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4719" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115074 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the product $E_1 E_{2}^{-1},$ where ![](images/c2/c26c31fb0c03ed31508d71c394a0cac97706b8e98f495758ef1bc3cb7b9de37f.svg) and ![](images/27/2728c629af340140b991c75f58104833304f4dc82d6f2f04b29b6ddde363b349.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115074 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ed71f469-f0e9-5016-846d-cb70d59ab3ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115074 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that $E_1$ and $E_2$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find $E_2^{-1}.$ We write down $E_2$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n![](images/11/11f12b427f63eb036d2f19210ab85c0a5b279bfda547031e8ae867d130aafc15.svg)\n\nNow, we can find the product $E_1 E_{2}^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/4c/4c46704dbedb6bb10fd40107493f49481923b4f1e524dc8f3f84d98aa4f66256.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186115243 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115243 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115243 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115243 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115243 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186265973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115243 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115243 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115243 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115243 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115243 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115243 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115243 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115243 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115243 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115243 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying an Elementary 2x2 Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115243 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186115072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115243 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "853a04b2-591a-51c2-b80f-73f559355d9f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115244 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186265975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115244 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Inverse of an Elementary 2x2 Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115244 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186115073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115244 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6c56b3e9-cf23-5e1e-8c7d-c474059c9a9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115245 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186265980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186265982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115245 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing a Product Involving the Inverse of an Elementary 2x2 Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115245 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186115074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115245 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "dd12bc7b-1b5d-5af2-bcef-c9d0fda7d8fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115472 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115472 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115472 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115472 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115472 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115472 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115472 #edb/ref 1045 "Elementary 2x2 Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115472 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186116124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115472 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115472 #edb/ref 1050 480.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115472 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "0ae25db9-bfdf-5334-a08b-5c0545c2dcde" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115841 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186113983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115841 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42696" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115841 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115841 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115841 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e24eefb5-7086-59e4-b488-5ab843ea61ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115841 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{1}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{1}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{1}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+7{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}15 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116124 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116124 #edb/ref 1061 17220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116124 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ffae07b2-82f2-5c26-9de5-cb69f517ee2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116124 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116125 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116125 #edb/ref 1061 17221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116125 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7e9e3e32-4737-52d8-8430-910568cdf491" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116125 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116126 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116126 #edb/ref 1061 17223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116126 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d5bdf1c7-f166-5b10-9f1a-0aab443b2ea0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116126 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116127 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116127 #edb/ref 1061 17224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116127 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9582140d-32a5-56c0-81bf-8a788ac9965a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116127 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116128 #edb/ref 1061 17225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116128 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "842b6803-5ec6-5291-b4c6-ea03cd8e381f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116128 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116495 #edb/ref 1064 "An **elementary matrix** is a matrix that can be obtained from the identity matrix by applying *only one* elementary row operation.\n\nFor example, starting with the $2 \\times 2$ identity matrix ![](images/09/09dfa5716dbd500a4684f62a5c409a392761e7546c7ac5e4b592133623552506.svg) we can apply a single elementary row operation to get any of the matrices below:\n\n![](images/44/44bf82527f952ba4d01bbc16f0b81a47fe9e82f447a996f75bccef5171d92242.svg)\n\n- $E_1$ is obtained from $I_2$ by applying the row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2.$\n\n- $E_2$ is obtained from $I_2$ by applying the row operation $R_1:=2R_1.$\n\n- $E_3$ is obtained from $I_2$ by applying the row operation $R_2 := R_2 + (-7)R_1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116495 #edb/ref 1065 3164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116495 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "85735558-3e6a-5a6e-be0b-95227712e60a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116495 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116496 #edb/ref 1064 "Each elementary matrix $E$ is invertible, and the corresponding inverse is always an elementary matrix of the same type as $E$.\nTo illustrate, let's consider the matrix \n\n![](images/01/0144b651cc6eff46265156e1491f58aacfc167da6dce571dbf4a92c9d94e7177.svg)\nTo find the inverse of $E,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1:** Write down $\\color{blue}E$ and the identity matrix $I_2$ side-by-side, as shown below:\n![](images/fb/fbb291c4f2d7ccc79939cf9368579b07f6649709f123fb70a1383deefaec6fc1.svg)\n\n**Step 2:** Apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix. In our case, we need to apply the transformation $R_2 := R_2 + (-4)R_1.$ We apply the operation to the entire row (not only the left part):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,{I}_{2}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[{I}_{2}\\,|\\,{E}^{-1}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $E^{-1}$ is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore,\n![](images/1e/1e71a52dda452f98fa77c01117f40d055fa0661100a0e4ffc25147d9baefc0af.svg)\n\nWe can work out the inverse of all elementary $2 \\times 2$ matrices in a similar way." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116496 #edb/ref 1065 3165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116496 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "24a632e9-4a94-5212-b848-8d4b4403d452" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116496 #edb/ref 1067 "The Inverse of an Elementary Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263039 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}21 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186263039 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263039 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0449b553-1b65-5cac-8598-b0d8a270a950" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263084 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186263084 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263084 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "08cf208e-4a67-56fb-af8e-a3fd93304b09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264057 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186264057 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264057 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "79e21f99-e8cc-51c3-a318-a7ddd869b864" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264091 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186264091 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264091 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7e60b2a6-6056-5ea2-8a17-c6be4b8de34e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264350 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}20 & -7 \\\\ 8 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186264350 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264350 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9e4de5ce-9af6-520d-b7aa-ae9f2db45567" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265636 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265636 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265636 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265636 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265636 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265636 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265636 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265636 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186263039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265636 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dcf3b9f2-af48-548d-9af3-5530034cb7ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265973 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186464883 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186265973 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186464884 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186265973 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186464885 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186265973 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130774" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265973 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265973 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186265973 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6ec5e472-dc1d-5891-bb93-866a6d9b3914" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265973 #edb/ref 1074 "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $B$ are elementary matrices." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186265982 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265982 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130823" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265982 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265982 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{E}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186265982 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d8b759c1-7acc-5a06-82c3-b179ddb571b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265982 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{2}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{2}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{2}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}21 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186388160 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186388161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388160 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130798" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388160 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388160 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186388160 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6550d7b1-7dc0-5855-b6b5-da0256aaa98e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388160 #edb/ref 1074 "We write down $Q$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Q\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{7} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{1}{7}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{7} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{Q}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{1}{7} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186388161 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388161 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388161 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388161 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388161 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388161 #edb/ref 1076 #